WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.508 --> 00:00:03.145
- I don't think you determine
what your purpose is,

00:00:03.145 --> 00:00:05.537
I think you discover what your purpose is.

00:00:05.537 --> 00:00:06.569
There's a difference.

00:00:06.569 --> 00:00:09.299
This is all we've got,
right here, right now.

00:00:09.299 --> 00:00:11.549
We don't know how much we
have left in the future,

00:00:11.549 --> 00:00:13.427
but we do know what we've got now.

00:00:13.427 --> 00:00:15.659
95% of the people live their entire life

00:00:15.659 --> 00:00:17.207
and never live the way they want to live.

00:00:17.207 --> 00:00:20.104
Do you know that the law of
attraction is always working?

00:00:20.104 --> 00:00:21.642
It's like the law of gravity.

00:00:21.642 --> 00:00:24.422
If I let this go, it's going to go down.

00:00:24.422 --> 00:00:27.025
It's never going to go back
up; it's going to go down.

00:00:27.025 --> 00:00:29.526
- He's widely regarded as
one of the living masters

00:00:29.526 --> 00:00:31.705
and teachers of the law of attraction.

00:00:31.705 --> 00:00:34.590
For over 50 years, he's
transformed the lives of people

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.233
through his books, seminars,
and personal coaching.

00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:39.320
He worked shoulder-to-shoulder
with Earl Nightingale,

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:40.799
in the 60s and 70s.

00:00:40.799 --> 00:00:42.618
He's Bob Proctor, and here's my take

00:00:42.618 --> 00:00:44.528
on his top 10 rules for success.

00:00:44.528 --> 00:00:46.269
Rule number five is my personal favorite,

00:00:46.269 --> 00:00:48.162
and make sure to stick around
all through 'till the end

00:00:48.162 --> 00:00:49.647
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:49.647 --> 00:00:51.687
And as always, if Bob says
something that really, really

00:00:51.687 --> 00:00:53.962
resonates with you, please
leave it down in the comments

00:00:53.962 --> 00:00:55.506
below, and put quotes around it

00:00:55.506 --> 00:00:57.771
so other people can be inspired as well.

00:00:57.771 --> 00:00:58.604
Enjoy.

00:01:01.704 --> 00:01:05.287
(dramatic orchestra music)

00:01:11.352 --> 00:01:14.207
- I don't think you determine
what your purpose is,

00:01:14.207 --> 00:01:16.639
I think you discover what your purpose is.

00:01:16.639 --> 00:01:17.880
There's a difference.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.463
Determining indicates deciding,

00:01:23.398 --> 00:01:25.033
and I don't think you decide.

00:01:25.033 --> 00:01:27.164
I think, if you go about it the right way,

00:01:27.164 --> 00:01:28.973
you discover it.

00:01:28.973 --> 00:01:32.961
Like, there's some people that
should be painting all day;

00:01:32.961 --> 00:01:34.853
they're great artists,

00:01:34.853 --> 00:01:38.008
like Michelangelo, who was
obviously a great artist,

00:01:38.008 --> 00:01:39.425
a great sculptor.

00:01:40.416 --> 00:01:42.749
I mean, that was his purpose

00:01:44.508 --> 00:01:46.560
in his life.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:51.193
Well, I believe my purpose
is doing what I'm doing.

00:01:51.193 --> 00:01:54.794
Your purpose is why you get
out of bed in the morning.

00:01:54.794 --> 00:01:56.330
Do you know why you get up?

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:58.083
Well, most people say,
well it's to go to work.

00:01:58.083 --> 00:02:00.404
Well that'd be a good
reason to stay in bed.

00:02:00.404 --> 00:02:03.178
You know, you say, well
everybody's doing it.

00:02:03.178 --> 00:02:05.078
That'd be another reason to stay in bed.

00:02:05.078 --> 00:02:06.650
If you're ever doing
what everybody's doing,

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:09.756
you're probably going
in the wrong direction.

00:02:09.756 --> 00:02:12.713
Your purpose is your reason for living.

00:02:12.713 --> 00:02:16.571
What you want to do is
sit down, and maybe,

00:02:16.571 --> 00:02:20.554
take a pen and a pad,

00:02:20.554 --> 00:02:24.232
and then ask yourself: what
do I really love doing?

00:02:24.232 --> 00:02:27.102
Now you may have to spend a while at this.

00:02:27.102 --> 00:02:30.734
You might get up an hour
early every morning,

00:02:30.734 --> 00:02:33.733
and go sit under a tree somewhere,

00:02:33.733 --> 00:02:35.979
if you're in a nice climate,

00:02:35.979 --> 00:02:38.420
or pick a favorite chair, some place

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:40.876
where you're not going to be disturbed,

00:02:40.876 --> 00:02:44.376
and totally relax, and say:

00:02:45.826 --> 00:02:49.657
if I could spend my life doing something,

00:02:49.657 --> 00:02:51.990
what do I really love doing?

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.985
Now since you don't ask yourself
that question every day,

00:02:55.985 --> 00:02:59.581
it might take a while for the
answer to come to the surface.

00:02:59.581 --> 00:03:03.276
But it comes to your consciousness,

00:03:03.276 --> 00:03:05.329
and it may take a while;
you may have to do this

00:03:05.329 --> 00:03:07.912
every morning for three months.

00:03:09.013 --> 00:03:12.708
But it would be well-invested, the time.

00:03:12.708 --> 00:03:14.781
How many times have you
had somebody say to you:

00:03:14.781 --> 00:03:16.971
hey, have you got a minute, Bob?

00:03:16.971 --> 00:03:18.871
Have you got a minute?

00:03:18.871 --> 00:03:20.762
They don't want a minute.

00:03:20.762 --> 00:03:23.086
I want you to think about that.

00:03:23.086 --> 00:03:25.038
I read a poem one time, it said:

00:03:25.038 --> 00:03:29.575
I have only just a minute,
only 60 seconds and

00:03:29.575 --> 00:03:31.457
it was forced upon me, I can't refuse it.

00:03:31.457 --> 00:03:33.497
I didn't seek it and I didn't choose it,

00:03:33.497 --> 00:03:35.481
but it's up to me to use it.

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:37.272
I must suffer if I lose it,

00:03:37.272 --> 00:03:39.076
give account if I abuse it.

00:03:39.076 --> 00:03:43.169
It's only a tiny little
minute, but eternity is in it.

00:03:43.169 --> 00:03:45.893
Well any rate, I thought,
I'm going to put this

00:03:45.893 --> 00:03:47.964
on a video, and I'm just going
to share it with everybody,

00:03:47.964 --> 00:03:51.784
just this one little part
of the seminar I'm making.

00:03:51.784 --> 00:03:54.294
I want you to look at this
sand timer, and let this,

00:03:54.294 --> 00:03:56.007
I want you to think about your life.

00:03:56.007 --> 00:03:59.596
The sand in the top of the
glass represents the future.

00:03:59.596 --> 00:04:03.220
The sand in the bottom of the
glass represents the past.

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:05.464
Now, do you know, we
know what's in the past,

00:04:05.464 --> 00:04:07.478
and yet, we can't do anything about it.

00:04:07.478 --> 00:04:08.922
Do you know what the trick is?

00:04:08.922 --> 00:04:12.129
You don't know how much sand's
in the top of the glass.

00:04:12.129 --> 00:04:14.851
You may think you have a lot,
you might only have a little.

00:04:14.851 --> 00:04:17.235
Then again, you may
think you have a little,

00:04:17.235 --> 00:04:19.038
and you might have a lot.

00:04:19.038 --> 00:04:22.029
When I was a little boy, I
was raised by my grandmother.

00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:24.126
And, I don't know, when
I was little, after,

00:04:24.126 --> 00:04:27.323
she was probably 60, but,
as long as I could remember,

00:04:27.323 --> 00:04:29.190
grandma would say, I'll
soon be gone, dear,

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:30.626
I'll soon be gone.

00:04:30.626 --> 00:04:32.115
Well, you know, we loved her.

00:04:32.115 --> 00:04:34.049
She was really an angel
of God, but we thought

00:04:34.049 --> 00:04:35.465
she was never going to go.

00:04:35.465 --> 00:04:39.459
She lived to be 94; for 30 some years:

00:04:39.459 --> 00:04:40.959
I'll soon be gone.

00:04:42.042 --> 00:04:43.512
She didn't think she had much time left;

00:04:43.512 --> 00:04:46.159
she had 34 years left.

00:04:46.159 --> 00:04:48.620
Now, about the same time,
I had a buddy of mine,

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:50.685
Bob Yates, he was just 16,

00:04:50.685 --> 00:04:53.197
and bang, he run into
the abutment of a bridge,

00:04:53.197 --> 00:04:55.131
and his life was snuffed out.

00:04:55.131 --> 00:04:57.442
If you had asked him a half hour before,

00:04:57.442 --> 00:04:59.483
how much sand he had
left, he would've said

00:04:59.483 --> 00:05:01.252
at least a half a century.

00:05:01.252 --> 00:05:02.980
He didn't have a half an hour.

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:06.380
See, the trick is, we don't
know how much sand we have left.

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:10.063
The future, we don't know.

00:05:10.063 --> 00:05:11.508
The past, it's gone.

00:05:11.508 --> 00:05:13.278
Now, the only thing you can deal with

00:05:13.278 --> 00:05:15.397
is what's right here, right now.

00:05:15.397 --> 00:05:19.293
And if you look, the
sand is always moving.

00:05:19.293 --> 00:05:21.002
Now think of this:

00:05:21.002 --> 00:05:23.692
you see, I was working on that, and then,

00:05:23.692 --> 00:05:27.135
I was working on a graph
I've got here on my computer,

00:05:27.135 --> 00:05:28.899
and I'm thinking of the time somebody said

00:05:28.899 --> 00:05:30.889
hey, have you got a minute?

00:05:30.889 --> 00:05:33.386
Well they don't really want a minute.

00:05:33.386 --> 00:05:35.394
Do you know, if you're
earning $50,000 a year,

00:05:35.394 --> 00:05:37.803
a minute's 42 cents?

00:05:37.803 --> 00:05:40.944
A half hour is $12.50.

00:05:40.944 --> 00:05:42.985
If we take that ahead a
bit, if you're earning

00:05:42.985 --> 00:05:45.985
$80,000 a year, a minute's 67 cents.

00:05:47.021 --> 00:05:48.438
Half hour is $20.

00:05:50.125 --> 00:05:52.817
If you're earning $150,000 a year,

00:05:52.817 --> 00:05:54.373
you got a minute?
(laughing)

00:05:54.373 --> 00:05:57.289
It costs you $1.25 a minute,

00:05:57.289 --> 00:06:00.351
and a half hour is $37.50.

00:06:00.351 --> 00:06:01.637
So do you see, if somebody said:

00:06:01.637 --> 00:06:04.131
do you want to stop for a cup of coffee?

00:06:04.131 --> 00:06:07.338
Well, if you're earning a
quarter of a million dollars

00:06:07.338 --> 00:06:11.288
a year, a minute is $2.08,

00:06:11.288 --> 00:06:13.955
half hour is $62.50.

00:06:15.725 --> 00:06:17.188
And then of course, you've got the coffee

00:06:17.188 --> 00:06:19.414
to pay for, on top of that.

00:06:19.414 --> 00:06:22.340
So you see, the point is this:

00:06:22.340 --> 00:06:25.396
this is all we've got,
right here, right now.

00:06:25.396 --> 00:06:27.640
We don't know how much we
have left in the future,

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:29.737
but we do know what we've got now,

00:06:29.737 --> 00:06:32.414
and I have found that people that win

00:06:32.414 --> 00:06:34.605
are the people who make up their mind;

00:06:34.605 --> 00:06:36.291
they're not going to waste the minutes.

00:06:36.291 --> 00:06:37.932
They're going to be
productive; they're going to

00:06:37.932 --> 00:06:40.317
make it happen, every minute.

00:06:40.317 --> 00:06:44.102
Success was the progressive
realization of a worthy ideal.

00:06:44.102 --> 00:06:46.547
Anyone that has a goal, and
they're moving towards it,

00:06:46.547 --> 00:06:47.823
they're successful.

00:06:47.823 --> 00:06:50.199
Most people think that
your successful if you have

00:06:50.199 --> 00:06:52.315
a lot of money; quite often,
you have a lot of money

00:06:52.315 --> 00:06:54.062
if you're successful, but it isn't,

00:06:54.062 --> 00:06:57.790
I wouldn't say Mother Teresa
has a lot of money, you know,

00:06:57.790 --> 00:07:00.089
but she's a pretty successful lady.

00:07:00.089 --> 00:07:01.689
So it's--

00:07:01.689 --> 00:07:03.470
- You said that barrier to success--

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:04.842
- Well there's a couple of them.

00:07:04.842 --> 00:07:05.827
I think there's two barriers.

00:07:05.827 --> 00:07:07.747
One is our conditioning; the conditioning

00:07:07.747 --> 00:07:10.306
that takes place in our subconscious mind,

00:07:10.306 --> 00:07:12.267
from the time we're infants:

00:07:12.267 --> 00:07:16.342
all we can do is act and talk
like the people around us.

00:07:16.342 --> 00:07:18.546
That's why we learned
the language we learned.

00:07:18.546 --> 00:07:20.598
If there was 10 languages
spoken in our home,

00:07:20.598 --> 00:07:23.677
we'd learn 10 languages,
without any trouble.

00:07:23.677 --> 00:07:25.923
There's usually one, and that's
the only one we ever learn,

00:07:25.923 --> 00:07:27.049
and we grow older and we think,

00:07:27.049 --> 00:07:28.471
oh, I couldn't learn another language.

00:07:28.471 --> 00:07:31.071
We could learn 100 if we
wanted to, you can do anything,

00:07:31.071 --> 00:07:32.333
but I think we're conditioned.

00:07:32.333 --> 00:07:34.860
We have a real strong conditioning,

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:38.199
usually, with not some very good ideas.

00:07:38.199 --> 00:07:41.631
And then, that's the
barrier that's inside us.

00:07:41.631 --> 00:07:43.835
The one that's outside
of us is our environment.

00:07:43.835 --> 00:07:47.827
We have a tendency to act
like everybody around us,

00:07:47.827 --> 00:07:49.462
and if you think about this,

00:07:49.462 --> 00:07:51.046
it doesn't make a lot of sense,

00:07:51.046 --> 00:07:54.093
because, if you study
statistics, 95% of the people

00:07:54.093 --> 00:07:55.593
live their entire life, and never live

00:07:55.593 --> 00:07:57.676
the way they want to live.

00:07:57.676 --> 00:08:00.360
Do you know that 95% of the
population, in this country,

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.004
let's say in North America,

00:08:02.004 --> 00:08:04.371
the richest continent in
the history of the world,

00:08:04.371 --> 00:08:07.201
they'll work productively,
let's say for 40

00:08:07.201 --> 00:08:11.368
of their 65 years, and they'll
end up with hardly any money.

00:08:12.346 --> 00:08:14.850
There's got to be something wrong.

00:08:14.850 --> 00:08:17.616
Five percent of the
people end up financially

00:08:17.616 --> 00:08:20.430
comfortable or independent.

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:21.887
- Are you trying to depress us, Bob?

00:08:21.887 --> 00:08:23.328
Because that's--
- No, actually,

00:08:23.328 --> 00:08:25.809
I think it's quite an exciting idea,

00:08:25.809 --> 00:08:30.252
because you see, the idea behind
it is that anybody can win.

00:08:30.252 --> 00:08:31.522
Anyone at all.

00:08:31.522 --> 00:08:33.373
But if we start studying these statistics,

00:08:33.373 --> 00:08:34.880
I think we'll arrive at the conclusion,

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:36.584
geez, I better start thinking for myself

00:08:36.584 --> 00:08:38.465
rather than follow everybody.

00:08:38.465 --> 00:08:40.170
Most people, they get a
job, they look around,

00:08:40.170 --> 00:08:41.908
you see how everybody
else is doing their work,

00:08:41.908 --> 00:08:43.659
and they start doing it the same way.

00:08:43.659 --> 00:08:44.964
They should stop and
think: I wonder if any

00:08:44.964 --> 00:08:46.735
of these people know what they're doing?

00:08:46.735 --> 00:08:48.634
You know, is there a better way to do it?

00:08:48.634 --> 00:08:50.328
- Don't we have a need to fit in?

00:08:50.328 --> 00:08:52.125
I mean, we don't want to
stand out, we don't want

00:08:52.125 --> 00:08:54.596
to get fired, we don't want to make waves.

00:08:54.596 --> 00:08:55.580
- Exactly.

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:57.407
Exactly, just fall into line, you know,

00:08:57.407 --> 00:08:59.467
take a number, be like everybody around.

00:08:59.467 --> 00:09:02.678
You know, that'd be great
in the animal kingdom,

00:09:02.678 --> 00:09:05.062
but human beings aren't
supposed to live that way.

00:09:05.062 --> 00:09:07.051
I think we should make a few waves.

00:09:07.051 --> 00:09:08.889
We should maybe stand out, be different,

00:09:08.889 --> 00:09:10.970
not for the sake of being different,

00:09:10.970 --> 00:09:13.206
but because we are different.

00:09:13.206 --> 00:09:15.624
We all think different thoughts,

00:09:15.624 --> 00:09:17.915
and I believe we should start to think,

00:09:17.915 --> 00:09:20.776
and build images in our mind,
of what we'd like to do,

00:09:20.776 --> 00:09:22.579
and then set out and do it.

00:09:22.579 --> 00:09:24.316
Emerson did that, Edison did that,

00:09:24.316 --> 00:09:27.093
Marconi did that, Samuel Morse did that,

00:09:27.093 --> 00:09:31.032
Buckminster Fuller did that,
we could go on, and on, and on.

00:09:31.032 --> 00:09:32.395
They were different, they stood out,

00:09:32.395 --> 00:09:33.863
they made a few waves.

00:09:33.863 --> 00:09:36.585
You should seek failures.

00:09:36.585 --> 00:09:40.817
See, if you're playing it
safe, you're not going to win.

00:09:40.817 --> 00:09:44.066
If you're playing it safe,
like that old saying,

00:09:44.066 --> 00:09:45.816
you know, you were taught
when you were a little girl:

00:09:45.816 --> 00:09:47.200
better to be safe than sorry.

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:48.582
Well that's a bunch of crap.

00:09:48.582 --> 00:09:52.361
It's not better to be safe than sorry.

00:09:52.361 --> 00:09:54.891
It's by trying things that you figure out

00:09:54.891 --> 00:09:57.183
how far you can go.

00:09:57.183 --> 00:09:59.926
You got to get outside the box.

00:09:59.926 --> 00:10:03.767
Like it's reported,
Edison tried 3,000 ways

00:10:03.767 --> 00:10:05.719
before he built the incandescent light.

00:10:05.719 --> 00:10:08.619
He said, he didn't fail 3,000 times,

00:10:08.619 --> 00:10:10.865
he said there was 3,000 different steps

00:10:10.865 --> 00:10:13.678
to building a light bulb.

00:10:13.678 --> 00:10:16.798
Well there's different steps
to get to where you're going.

00:10:16.798 --> 00:10:19.378
And I really do treat winning and losing

00:10:19.378 --> 00:10:23.053
exactly the same; I do
not let it upset me.

00:10:23.053 --> 00:10:24.459
- Do you think that's a big part

00:10:24.459 --> 00:10:26.691
of why you're so successful?

00:10:26.691 --> 00:10:30.123
- Well, it's part of why I enjoy it.

00:10:30.123 --> 00:10:33.935
Yeah, I think it is,
'cause I try anything.

00:10:33.935 --> 00:10:37.158
If I'm into an idea, the idea of losing

00:10:37.158 --> 00:10:38.908
never enters my mind.

00:10:39.814 --> 00:10:43.573
See, I have worked with giants.

00:10:43.573 --> 00:10:48.410
I had the good fortune of
working with Sir Edmund Hillary.

00:10:48.410 --> 00:10:52.460
He was the first man to
stand on top of the world,

00:10:52.460 --> 00:10:56.977
and I worked with him on
three different occasions,

00:10:56.977 --> 00:10:59.971
and the only difference in
Ed Hillary and Bob Proctor

00:10:59.971 --> 00:11:01.708
is in his size.

00:11:01.708 --> 00:11:04.480
Now, he's gone now, but he was a big man.

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:06.108
When he shook hands with me, his hand

00:11:06.108 --> 00:11:09.841
wrapped right around
mine; I felt like a dwarf.

00:11:09.841 --> 00:11:12.887
You know, and, in talking to him,

00:11:12.887 --> 00:11:14.520
like it never entered his mind he wouldn't

00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:15.983
get to the top of the mountain,

00:11:15.983 --> 00:11:19.816
but he failed in 1951, he failed in 1952,

00:11:20.852 --> 00:11:23.111
and it wasn't until 1953 that he got

00:11:23.111 --> 00:11:24.338
to the top of the mountain.

00:11:24.338 --> 00:11:27.935
You know, people had died for
centuries, trying to do that.

